{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_37a82845ea3e4d0c88fbf309","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now read all 31 TypeScript source files, 3 JSON data files, and the vite.config.ts. Here is the comprehensive analysis.\n\n---\n\n# Horror Sim Game Project -- Full Code Review\n\n## Files Read (35 total)\n\n**Source files (31 .ts):**\n- `src/game/StoreMap.ts`\n- `src/game/Player.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Camera.ts`\n- `src/game/ProductManager.ts`\n- `src/game/Customer.ts`\n- `src/game/CustomerManager.ts`\n- `src/game/CashRegister.ts`\n- `src/game/PricingSystem.ts`\n- `src/game/EconomySystem.ts`\n- `src/game/ShelfInteraction.ts`\n- `src/game/DayCycle.ts`\n- `src/game/DaySummary.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Lighting.ts`\n- `src/game/UpgradeManager.ts`\n- `src/game/SaveManager.ts`\n- `src/game/BehaviorTree.ts`\n- `src/game/JumpScareManager.ts`\n- `src/game/HorrorEventManager.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Renderer.ts`\n- `src/game/NarrativeTypes.ts`\n- `src/game/StoryManager.ts`\n- `src/game/EndingSystem.ts`\n- `src/game/NoteManager.ts`\n- `src/game/NarrativeChoiceManager.ts`\n- `src/game/GameState.ts`\n- `src/game/ClueManager.ts`\n- `src/game/RadioManager.ts`\n- `src/game/AudioManager.ts`\n- `src/main/main.ts`\n- `src/main/preload.ts`\n- `src/renderer/App.ts`\n- `vite.config.ts`\n\n**Data files (3 .json):**\n- `src/data/products.json`\n- `src/data/suppliers.json`\n- `src/data/upgrades.json`\n\n**Test files: NONE** -- the `tests/` directory is empty.\n\n---\n\n## Per-File Analysis\n\n### 1. StoreMap.ts\n**Purpose:** 30x25 tile map of the convenience store with collision detection and tile rendering.\n**Key items:** `StoreMap` class, `T` tile enum, `STORE_LAYOUT` 2D array.\n**Issues:**\n- The wall brick pattern detail rendering (line 233) has a minor visual artifact: when `gy % 2 !== 0`, both horizontal and vertical lines are drawn at similar positions, creating an inconsistent brick pattern.\n- Row 12 has `0,0` tiles on the left side (columns 25-28), creating a gap in the bottom-left corner wall. This means the store has an odd open area in the lower-left that has no wall tile, which looks like a map design error.\n\n### 2. Player.ts\n**Purpose:** Player character with WASD movement, collision, and pixel-art rendering.\n**Key items:** `Player` class, `PLAYER_SIZE=20`, `getFacingTile()`.\n**Issues:**\n- `toState()` hardcodes `money: 0, debt: 0, day: 1, health: 100, sanity: 100` -- these are not the player's actual values. This means the PlayerState returned is incorrect for all fields except position/speed/direction. This is a real bug since `toState()` could be used for save/load.\n- The interaction indicator (\"E\" hint) is always shown even when no interactable is nearby. It should be conditional.\n\n### 3. Camera.ts\n**Purpose:** Smooth-follow camera with zoom (1x-4x), screen-to-world coordinate conversion.\n**Key items:** `Camera` class, `follow()`, `zoomAt()`.\n**Issues:** None significant. Well-implemented.\n\n### 4. ProductManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Product catalog, inventory, supplier ordering, shelf management.\n**Key items:** `ProductManager` class, `orderProduct()`, `stockShelf()`, `expireProducts()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Expiry logic bug:** When adding to an existing inventory item, if the existing item already has an expiry date, the new expiry is NOT updated (line 159-161). This means mixing old and fresh stock keeps the old expiry, causing premature expiration of new stock or keeping expired stock valid.\n- `storageCapacity` is used as per-item max, but the GDD says storage is total capacity. The `maxQuantity` on each inventory item equals `storageCapacity` (100), meaning each product can hold up to 100 individually, for a theoretical total of thousands. This contradicts the `storageUsed` calculation which sums all quantities. The system allows stocking beyond storage capacity if checking only `storageCapacity` per item.\n\n### 5. Customer.ts\n**Purpose:** Individual customer entity with waypoint navigation, behavior states, and pixel rendering.\n**Key items:** `Customer` class, `CustomerState` enum, 5 customer types.\n**Issues:**\n- **No category filtering in `decideWhatToBuy()`:** The method pushes ANY shelf with products as a candidate, ignoring `preferredCategories`. A commuter looking for beverages could end up browsing the daily goods shelf. The category filtering is commented out (\"simplified: check by shelf type\").\n- The `isStealing()` method is called externally but never triggers a theft event. There is no code path where a student actually steals -- the method exists but is never integrated.\n- Price is set to 0 in the cart when picking (`price: 0`), only set at checkout. This is fine architecturally but means the customer's budget is never checked during shopping -- they could pick items exceeding their budget.\n\n### 6. CustomerManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Spawns customers on schedule, manages lifecycle, tracks stats.\n**Key items:** `CustomerManager` class, `SPAWN_SCHEDULES`, `isInTimeRange()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Critical type error on line 25:** `customers: Customer[] = new Map() as unknown as Customer[];` -- This property is declared as an array but initialized as a Map cast to array. It is never actually used (the real collection is `activeCustomers: Map<string, Customer>`), but it is a dead field with a wrong type.\n- `totalRevenue` in `CustomerManager` tracks revenue separately from `EconomySystem.dailyRevenue`. These can diverge since `CustomerManager.recordSale()` takes a `total` parameter but this is only called externally if someone remembers to call it. The register transaction does NOT call `recordSale()` -- it calls `customer.finishCheckout()` directly. So `CustomerManager.totalRevenue` will always be 0.\n- Night customer spawn: `endHour: 2` with `isInTimeRange(23, 2)` works correctly via the midnight-wrap logic. Good.\n\n### 7. CashRegister.ts\n**Purpose:** Checkout state machine (scanning -> payment -> change -> receipt -> anomaly).\n**Key items:** `CashRegister` class, `Transaction` interface, `RegisterState` enum.\n**Issues:**\n- **Customer never finishes checkout on anomaly:** When `triggerAnomaly()` is called (line 173-196), the state changes to ANOMALY and after 2 seconds calls `reset()`. But `reset()` sets `currentCustomer = null` without calling `customer.finishCheckout()`. The customer is now stuck in `CHECKING_OUT` state forever, never completing or leaving. This is a **real bug** -- anomalous transactions leak customers.\n- `findProductShelf()` just picks the first shelf with the product, but if a customer picked from a specific shelf, it may deduct from a different shelf's stock.\n\n### 8. PricingSystem.ts\n**Purpose:** Dynamic pricing with time-of-day demand modifiers and price sensitivity.\n**Key items:** `PricingSystem` class, `updateDemand()`, `willCustomerBuy()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Demand-based product matching uses string includes:** Lines 66-81 check product IDs with `id.includes('water')`, `id.includes('bento')`, etc. This is fragile -- a product named \"water_bottle\" would match the beverage demand modifier incorrectly. Should use category-based matching.\n- `willCustomerBuy()` is never actually called during the customer's shopping flow. Customers pick items regardless of price, making the entire pricing sensitivity system unused.\n\n### 9. EconomySystem.ts\n**Purpose:** Money management, debt tracking, upgrade purchasing, day-end reports.\n**Key items:** `EconomySystem` class, `endDay()`, `isGameOver()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Double-dipping upgrades:** Both `EconomySystem` and `UpgradeManager` track purchased upgrades independently. The `App.ts` applies upgrades from both systems, creating a dual-track that could lead to inconsistencies. If a player buys via `purchaseUpgrade()` (economy) vs `purchaseUpgradeM3()` (upgrade manager), only one system records it.\n- `isGameOver()` checks `dayNumber > DEBT_DAYS` but DEBT_DAYS is 30 and days start at 1, so the check on day 31 is correct. However, the `endDay()` increments dayNumber AFTER the check would normally happen, so the timing is fine.\n\n### 10. ShelfInteraction.ts\n**Purpose:** Player-shelf interaction for stocking/unstocking products.\n**Key items:** `ShelfInteraction` class, `interactWithShelf()`, `getAvailableProductsForShelf()`.\n**Issues:** Minor -- the class is well-structured. No significant bugs.\n\n### 11. DayCycle.ts\n**Purpose:** Day/night cycle with configurable time speeds per phase.\n**Key items:** `DayCycle` class, `DAY_PHASES`, phase change and new day listeners.\n**Issues:**\n- **Time wrap uses hour 30:** `DAY_PHASES` defines danger as 26-29 and dawn as 29-30, then wraps at 30 back to 6. This is internally consistent but means `timeOfDay` ranges from 6 to 30 (not 0-24). The `getPhaseFromTime()` function in GameState.ts handles both conventions but uses `>= 23 || < 2` for NIGHT, which works because the modular time in DayCycle goes 23,24,25,26... then wraps. However, `CustomerManager.isInTimeRange()` uses raw `timeOfDay` values from 0-30 range, which could break spawn schedules if the night phase time values exceed expected ranges.\n- The `getDaylight()` function returns 0 for all hours between 18 and 6, which means there is no gradual dawn/dusk twilight. The GDD specifies gradual transitions.\n\n### 12. DaySummary.ts\n**Purpose:** Daily settlement overlay with income/expense breakdown.\n**Key items:** `DaySummaryManager` class, `render()`, Canvas UI.\n**Issues:** None significant. Clean implementation.\n\n### 13. Lighting.ts\n**Purpose:** 2D lighting system using offscreen canvas with radial gradient light sources.\n**Key items:** `Lighting` class, `getDaylightFactor()`, `getDarknessLevel()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Performance concern:** Every frame, the lighting canvas is resized (`this.lightCanvas.width = width`), which clears the canvas and triggers a re-allocation. This is expensive. The canvas should only be resized when the viewport actually changes.\n- The offscreen canvas is created once but never cached -- gradient objects are created every frame for every light. For 8 lights at 60fps, that is 480 gradient objects per second. Should cache gradients when positions don't change.\n- The `destination-out` composite mode works correctly for cutting light holes in the darkness overlay.\n\n### 14. UpgradeManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Shop upgrade purchase system (lights, storage, register speed, etc.).\n**Key items:** `UpgradeManager` class, upgrade definitions.\n**Issues:**\n- **Duplicate definitions:** Upgrades are defined both here (hardcoded) AND in `upgrades.json` (used by `EconomySystem`). The two lists have different structures and can drift. `UpgradeManager` has 6 items, while `upgrades.json` has 8 (includes radio and flashlight). These are never synchronized.\n\n### 15. SaveManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Auto-save/load to localStorage with system registration.\n**Key items:** `SaveManager` class, `register()`, `save()`, `restore()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Incomplete save:** Only saves M2-M3 systems. M4-M6 systems (HorrorEventManager, JumpScareManager, NPCManager, ClueManager, StoryManager, EndingSystem, RadioManager, NoteManager, NarrativeChoiceManager) are NOT registered with the SaveManager. This means loading a save loses all horror event state, narrative progress, clue discoveries, and story choices.\n- **Two competing save systems:** Both `SaveManager` and `GameState.ts` have `saveGame()`/`loadGame()` functions using different localStorage keys (`midnight_store_save_v3` vs `midnight_store_save`). The App creates both and both run auto-save intervals. This can cause conflicts.\n\n### 16. BehaviorTree.ts\n**Purpose:** Behavior tree system for NPC AI with Selector, Sequence, and action nodes.\n**Key items:** `BTNode`, `Selector`, `Sequence`, `HorrorNPC`, `NPCManager`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Null tree reference:** In `createMysteriousCustomerTree(null!)`, `createLandlordTree(null!)`, and `createHimTree(null!)`, the NPC parameter is `null!` (force-unwrapped null). The tree actions reference `npc.state`, which would throw if `npc` were null. This works only because the closures capture the variable by reference and the NPC is assigned later. However, the `this.npcs` array creates NPCs with `null!` trees but the actual tree is set in the constructor. Wait -- actually, the tree IS passed in the constructor but the `npc` variable used inside closures refers to the outer `null!`. This is a real null pointer issue waiting to happen. The trees close over `npc` which is `null!` at creation time. The closures capture the variable binding, not the value, so when the tree ticks, `npc` is still the parameter value passed at construction... which is `null!`. **This is a bug.** The trees reference `npc.state` but `npc` is the parameter passed as `null!`, not the NPC being constructed.\n- Actually, re-reading: the trees are created as `createMysteriousCustomerTree(null!)` and then passed to the `HorrorNPC` constructor. The tree actions close over the `npc` parameter of the factory function, which is `null!`. These closures will throw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null` when ticked. This is a **confirmed bug**.\n\n### 17. JumpScareManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Jump scare system with daily limits (max 2), preview/scare/fade phases, and consequences.\n**Key items:** `JumpScareManager` class, `JumpScareType` enum, 5 scare types.\n**Issues:**\n- The `updateScare()` method uses `SCARE_DEFS.find(d => d.type === scare.type)!.scareDuration` which could crash if the definition is somehow missing. Minor since the type enum is controlled.\n- Otherwise well-designed. Respects the GDD's 2-scare-per-day limit and cooldown rules.\n\n### 18. HorrorEventManager.ts\n**Purpose:** The core horror system with 20 event types across 4 levels, visual state management, fear regulation.\n**Key items:** `HorrorEventManager` class, `HorrorEventType` enum, `HorrorVisualState`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Fear regulation works but is inverted:** `recentHighFearCount` is incremented when Level 3+ events trigger but is never decremented when time passes without high-fear events. The only reset is `resetDaily()`. Per the GDD, fear should recover gradually during gameplay, not only at day boundaries.\n- **probabilityMod can exceed reasonable bounds:** `dayProgress * 2 * nightDepth * 1.5` on Day 30 in DANGER phase gives `2.0 * 1.5 = 3.0`, tripling all base probabilities. Combined with multiple event types being checked per cycle, this means events fire much more frequently than the GDD's intended rates.\n- Otherwise very comprehensive -- matches the GDD's event list closely, implements buildup/active/resolve phases correctly.\n\n### 19. Renderer.ts\n**Purpose:** Main Canvas 2D renderer handling map, entities, HUD, panels, and horror visual effects.\n**Key items:** `Renderer` class, `render()`, `renderHorrorEffects()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Screen shake implementation is incorrect (line 683-685):** `c.drawImage(c.canvas, shakeX, shakeY)` draws the canvas onto itself at an offset. This creates feedback artifacts and does not produce proper screen shake. The correct approach is to apply the offset to all world-space rendering, or use `ctx.translate()` before rendering.\n- The renderer is nearly 800 lines and handles too many concerns (HUD, panels, horror effects). This should be split into multiple renderers.\n- The shelf panel does not handle the case where the `ShelfAction` is `STOCK` and the player wants to select which product -- the `stockShelf()` call from the UI is handled externally in `App.ts`, but there is no UI for selecting products within the panel itself.\n\n### 20. NarrativeTypes.ts\n**Purpose:** Shared type definitions for the narrative system.\n**Key items:** `StoryLayer`, `ClueType`, `ClueCategory`, `EndingType`, `NarrativeChoice`, `RadioSegment`, `StoryNote`.\n**Issues:**\n- Typo in `ClueCategory.PREVIOUS_CLERK` -- the value is `'previous_clerck'` (extra 'c'). This mismatch could cause issues if comparing by value rather than enum key.\n\n### 21. StoryManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 6-layer onion narrative progression with day-based layer unlocking.\n**Key items:** `StoryManager` class, layer transition logic.\n**Issues:**\n- **Bug in `acknowledgeEvent()`:** Line 131 filters the event from `pendingEvents`, then line 132 tries to find it in `pendingEvents` again (which was just filtered). The `find()` will always return `undefined`, so `undefined` gets pushed to `storyLog`. Should capture the event before filtering.\n\n### 22. EndingSystem.ts\n**Purpose:** 5 endings (Escape, Takeover, Resist, Merge, Truth) with clue/flag conditions.\n**Key items:** `EndingSystem` class, `evaluate()`, `forceEnding()`.\n**Issues:**\n- The Truth ending requires 65 clues, but `ClueManager` has exactly 75 total clues. Given that many clues have prerequisite chains and are gated behind specific actions (radio, notes), reaching 65 is extremely difficult. The ending conditions may need balancing.\n- Otherwise solid. The reverse-priority evaluation (checking best endings first) is correct.\n\n### 23. NoteManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 22 discoverable story notes across all 6 layers.\n**Key items:** `NoteManager` class, `ALL_NOTES` (22 notes).\n**Issues:** Clean implementation. Notes are well-written and match the GDD's narrative structure.\n\n### 24. NarrativeChoiceManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 10 story choices that set flags and affect endings.\n**Key items:** `NarrativeChoiceManager` class, 10 choices across all layers.\n**Issues:**\n- Choice `choice_final` (Day 30) has 5 options but the game's `forceEnding()` method does not use the final choice's result to determine the ending -- it uses accumulated flags. If the player hasn't set the right flags before Day 30, the final choice is cosmetic only.\n\n### 25. GameState.ts\n**Purpose:** Central game state interface, save/load functions, utility functions.\n**Key items:** `GameState` interface, `createInitialState()`, `getPhaseFromTime()`, `isNightTime()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **getPhaseFromTime() vs DayCycle mismatch:** `getPhaseFromTime()` treats time in 0-24 range, but `DayCycle` uses 6-30 range. For example, `getPhaseFromTime(25)` returns NIGHT (since `25 >= 23`), but in DayCycle, time 25 is in the DANGER phase. Since DayCycle stores its own phase, the `getPhaseFromTime()` in GameState is only used for initialization and should not cause runtime issues, but it is misleading.\n- `cloneState()` uses `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(state))` which will fail if `state` contains `Set`, `Map`, or circular references. The `GameState` interface does not contain these, so it works, but it is fragile.\n\n### 26. ClueManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 75 clues across 6 layers with prerequisite chains and auto-discovery.\n**Key items:** `ClueManager` class, `ALL_CLUES` (75 clues).\n**Issues:**\n- The clue IDs in the data reference IDs like `c_surf_03`, `c_first_01`, etc. These are cross-referenced by `NoteManager`, `RadioManager`, and `NarrativeChoiceManager`. I verified the referenced clue IDs exist in ALL_CLUES. This is correct.\n- `autoDiscoverForDay()` only auto-discovers ENVIRONMENT and CCTV type clues. NOTE, PRODUCT, RADIO, and CUSTOMER clues require explicit discovery, which is correct per the GDD.\n\n### 27. RadioManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 10 late-night radio segments that grant clues.\n**Key items:** `RadioManager` class, `RADIO_SEGMENTS`.\n**Issues:**\n- The `requiredHour` field uses hours in the 0-5 range (for the danger/night phase). But `DayCycle` uses time 23-30, while `RadioManager.update()` uses `timeOfDay % 24`. This means `requiredHour: 3.55` (radio_10) will correctly match `timeOfDay` values around 27.55 when modded to 3.55. This works.\n\n### 28. AudioManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Web Audio API sound system with SFX, ambient loops, BGM, and horror sounds.\n**Key items:** `AudioManager` class, ~20 sound methods, BGM generators.\n**Issues:**\n- **Memory leak:** Ambient and BGM oscillator nodes are pushed to arrays but only cleared on `stopAmbient()`/`stopBgm()`. If `playAmbient()` is called with the same type, it returns early (correct). But if the AudioContext is suspended and resumed, old nodes may not be properly garbage collected.\n- The audio is all synthesized (no sample files), which is fine for MVP but will not match the GDD's audio quality expectations for the final product.\n- BGM is very basic (3 sine waves for daytime, low drones for night). The GDD specifies distinct musical compositions with BPM and instruments. This is placeholder quality.\n\n### 29. main.ts\n**Purpose:** Electron main process with window creation and Steam API stub.\n**Key items:** `createWindow()`, Steam stub, IPC handlers.\n**Issues:**\n- `fullscreen` is imported from electron but never used.\n- Cloud save/load IPC handlers are stubs that do nothing. Fine for MVP.\n- `require('steamworks.js')` is wrapped in try/catch, which is correct for optional dependency.\n\n### 30. preload.ts\n**Purpose:** Electron context bridge exposing limited APIs to renderer.\n**Issues:** None. Minimal and secure.\n\n### 31. App.ts\n**Purpose:** Main game loop wiring all systems together.\n**Key items:** `App` class, `loop()`, system initialization.\n**Issues:**\n- **Two auto-save timers running simultaneously:** `startAutoSave()` (from GameState.ts) and `this.saveManager.startAutoSave()` are both called. They save to different localStorage keys and with potentially different data.\n- **M6 systems not saved:** The SaveManager only registers M2-M3 systems. ClueManager, StoryManager, EndingSystem, RadioManager, NoteManager, NarrativeChoiceManager, HorrorEventManager, JumpScareManager, and NPCManager are NOT registered. Loading a save loses all narrative progress.\n- **Day boundary timing:** `dayCycle.onNewDay()` triggers `this.onNewDay()` which calls `economy.endDay()`. But `economy.endDay()` increments the day counter. Meanwhile `dayCycle` also increments its own day counter. These could diverge since `endDay()` increments `economy.dayNumber` while `dayCycle` increments `this.day`.\n- **Unused purchaseUpgradeM3 method:** Line 693-702 provides an M3 upgrade path but the default is the M2 path. Dual code paths for upgrades is confusing.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Cutting Analysis\n\n### Save/Load Completeness\n**Status: INCOMPLETE.** Only M2-M3 systems are saved. M4-M6 (horror events, NPCs, jump scares, clues, story progress, notes, radio, endings, narrative choices) are all lost on save/load. This is the single biggest gap in the codebase.\n\n### Horror Event Trigger Logic\n**Status: MOSTLY CORRECT** with issues. The event system faithfully implements the GDD's 4-level structure, cooldown system, and sanity-based modifiers. Issues:\n- `recentHighFearCount` never decays during gameplay (only resets at day boundary).\n- Probability multipliers can stack too high (3x on Day 30 danger).\n- Events check all types every 5 seconds, which could cause burst triggers.\n\n### Lighting System Performance\n**Status: ACCEPTABLE for MVP, needs optimization.** The offscreen canvas approach is correct but:\n- Canvas is resized every frame (expensive).\n- Gradient objects are created per-light per-frame.\n- No culling of off-screen lights.\n- The system handles 8 lights at 60fps, which is fine for the current scale but will need optimization if more lights are added.\n\n### Narrative System Completeness\n**Status: COMPREHENSIVE.** The narrative system is the most complete part of the codebase:\n- 75 clues across 6 layers with prerequisite chains\n- 22 discoverable notes\n- 10 narrative choices\n- 10 radio segments\n- 5 endings with flag/clue requirements\n- 6-layer story progression\nAll narrative content is well-written, coherent, and closely matches the GDD's \"onion\" narrative design.\n\n### GDD Compliance\nThe code implements M1-M7 as planned. Notable deviations:\n- GDD specifies Howler.js for audio; the implementation uses raw Web Audio API instead.\n- GDD specifies WebGL for post-processing (glitch shader); the implementation uses Canvas 2D overlays.\n- GDD specifies `steamworks.js`; the implementation has a working stub.\n- GDD specifies `events.json`, `npcs.json`, `clues.json`, `endings.json` data files; only `products.json`, `suppliers.json`, and `upgrades.json` exist. The rest are hardcoded in TypeScript.\n\n### Test Coverage\n**Status: ZERO.** No test files exist. The `tests/` directory is empty. No unit tests, integration tests, or type-check tests were found.\n\n### Architecture Quality\n**Score: 6/10.** The codebase is well-organized into logical modules with clear responsibilities. The main issues are:\n1. Two competing save systems\n2. Dual upgrade tracking (EconomySystem vs UpgradeManager)\n3. NPC behavior tree null reference bug\n4. Customer leak on anomalous transactions\n5. M4-M6 systems not wired into save/load\n6. Renderer is a monolith (800+ lines)\n7. No dependency injection -- systems are tightly coupled via App.ts\n\n### Summary of Bugs Found\n\n| Severity | File | Bug |\n|----------|------|-----|\n| **Critical** | BehaviorTree.ts | NPC trees use `null!` reference -- all NPC behavior will crash on tick |\n| **Critical** | SaveManager.ts | M4-M6 systems not saved; narrative progress lost on load |\n| **High** | CashRegister.ts | Anomalous transactions leave customer stuck in CHECKING_OUT forever |\n| **High** | StoryManager.ts | `acknowledgeEvent()` pushes `undefined` to storyLog |\n| **High** | CustomerManager.ts | `totalRevenue` never updated (register does not call recordSale) |\n| **Medium** | ProductManager.ts | Expiry not updated when mixing old+new stock |\n| **Medium** | Player.ts | `toState()` returns hardcoded zeros for money/debt/health/sanity |\n| **Medium** | GameState.ts + SaveManager.ts | Two competing save systems with different keys |\n| **Medium** | Renderer.ts | Screen shake draws canvas onto itself (feedback artifacts) |\n| **Medium** | NarrativeTypes.ts | Typo `'previous_clerck'` in ClueCategory enum value |\n| **Low** | Customer.ts | Category filtering disabled -- customers browse any shelf |\n| **Low** | PricingSystem.ts | Demand matching uses fragile string includes |\n| **Low** | UpgradeManager.ts | Duplicate definitions with upgrades.json (8 vs 6 items) |\n| **Low** | DayCycle.ts | No gradual dawn/dusk (getDaylight returns 0 for 18-6) |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6fb83c653f7a353c (use SendMessage with to: 'a6fb83c653f7a353c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 144719\ntool_uses: 41\nduration_ms: 294425</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"561a5118-a4d0-464b-8f32-001690e29cb0","uuid":"8378cd83-5aaa-463c-b7cc-63fe2098c5ea","timestamp":"2026-05-23T10:17:31.876Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read ALL source files from the horror sim game project at D:/game-projects/steam-horror-sim/. \n\nFirst read GDD.md and package.json, then find and read all TypeScript source files in src/ directory and any test files in tests/ directory.\n\nFor each file report:\n- What it does\n- Key classes/functions/interfaces\n- Any bugs or issues found (type errors, logic errors, missing error handling, etc.)\n- Whether save/load is properly implemented\n- Whether the code matches the GDD design\n- Horror event trigger logic correctness\n- Lighting system performance concerns\n- Narrative system completeness\n\nAlso report the overall architecture quality and any cross-file inconsistencies. List all files you found and read.","agentId":"a6fb83c653f7a353c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now read all 31 TypeScript source files, 3 JSON data files, and the vite.config.ts. Here is the comprehensive analysis.\n\n---\n\n# Horror Sim Game Project -- Full Code Review\n\n## Files Read (35 total)\n\n**Source files (31 .ts):**\n- `src/game/StoreMap.ts`\n- `src/game/Player.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Camera.ts`\n- `src/game/ProductManager.ts`\n- `src/game/Customer.ts`\n- `src/game/CustomerManager.ts`\n- `src/game/CashRegister.ts`\n- `src/game/PricingSystem.ts`\n- `src/game/EconomySystem.ts`\n- `src/game/ShelfInteraction.ts`\n- `src/game/DayCycle.ts`\n- `src/game/DaySummary.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Lighting.ts`\n- `src/game/UpgradeManager.ts`\n- `src/game/SaveManager.ts`\n- `src/game/BehaviorTree.ts`\n- `src/game/JumpScareManager.ts`\n- `src/game/HorrorEventManager.ts`\n- `src/renderer/Renderer.ts`\n- `src/game/NarrativeTypes.ts`\n- `src/game/StoryManager.ts`\n- `src/game/EndingSystem.ts`\n- `src/game/NoteManager.ts`\n- `src/game/NarrativeChoiceManager.ts`\n- `src/game/GameState.ts`\n- `src/game/ClueManager.ts`\n- `src/game/RadioManager.ts`\n- `src/game/AudioManager.ts`\n- `src/main/main.ts`\n- `src/main/preload.ts`\n- `src/renderer/App.ts`\n- `vite.config.ts`\n\n**Data files (3 .json):**\n- `src/data/products.json`\n- `src/data/suppliers.json`\n- `src/data/upgrades.json`\n\n**Test files: NONE** -- the `tests/` directory is empty.\n\n---\n\n## Per-File Analysis\n\n### 1. StoreMap.ts\n**Purpose:** 30x25 tile map of the convenience store with collision detection and tile rendering.\n**Key items:** `StoreMap` class, `T` tile enum, `STORE_LAYOUT` 2D array.\n**Issues:**\n- The wall brick pattern detail rendering (line 233) has a minor visual artifact: when `gy % 2 !== 0`, both horizontal and vertical lines are drawn at similar positions, creating an inconsistent brick pattern.\n- Row 12 has `0,0` tiles on the left side (columns 25-28), creating a gap in the bottom-left corner wall. This means the store has an odd open area in the lower-left that has no wall tile, which looks like a map design error.\n\n### 2. Player.ts\n**Purpose:** Player character with WASD movement, collision, and pixel-art rendering.\n**Key items:** `Player` class, `PLAYER_SIZE=20`, `getFacingTile()`.\n**Issues:**\n- `toState()` hardcodes `money: 0, debt: 0, day: 1, health: 100, sanity: 100` -- these are not the player's actual values. This means the PlayerState returned is incorrect for all fields except position/speed/direction. This is a real bug since `toState()` could be used for save/load.\n- The interaction indicator (\"E\" hint) is always shown even when no interactable is nearby. It should be conditional.\n\n### 3. Camera.ts\n**Purpose:** Smooth-follow camera with zoom (1x-4x), screen-to-world coordinate conversion.\n**Key items:** `Camera` class, `follow()`, `zoomAt()`.\n**Issues:** None significant. Well-implemented.\n\n### 4. ProductManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Product catalog, inventory, supplier ordering, shelf management.\n**Key items:** `ProductManager` class, `orderProduct()`, `stockShelf()`, `expireProducts()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Expiry logic bug:** When adding to an existing inventory item, if the existing item already has an expiry date, the new expiry is NOT updated (line 159-161). This means mixing old and fresh stock keeps the old expiry, causing premature expiration of new stock or keeping expired stock valid.\n- `storageCapacity` is used as per-item max, but the GDD says storage is total capacity. The `maxQuantity` on each inventory item equals `storageCapacity` (100), meaning each product can hold up to 100 individually, for a theoretical total of thousands. This contradicts the `storageUsed` calculation which sums all quantities. The system allows stocking beyond storage capacity if checking only `storageCapacity` per item.\n\n### 5. Customer.ts\n**Purpose:** Individual customer entity with waypoint navigation, behavior states, and pixel rendering.\n**Key items:** `Customer` class, `CustomerState` enum, 5 customer types.\n**Issues:**\n- **No category filtering in `decideWhatToBuy()`:** The method pushes ANY shelf with products as a candidate, ignoring `preferredCategories`. A commuter looking for beverages could end up browsing the daily goods shelf. The category filtering is commented out (\"simplified: check by shelf type\").\n- The `isStealing()` method is called externally but never triggers a theft event. There is no code path where a student actually steals -- the method exists but is never integrated.\n- Price is set to 0 in the cart when picking (`price: 0`), only set at checkout. This is fine architecturally but means the customer's budget is never checked during shopping -- they could pick items exceeding their budget.\n\n### 6. CustomerManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Spawns customers on schedule, manages lifecycle, tracks stats.\n**Key items:** `CustomerManager` class, `SPAWN_SCHEDULES`, `isInTimeRange()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Critical type error on line 25:** `customers: Customer[] = new Map() as unknown as Customer[];` -- This property is declared as an array but initialized as a Map cast to array. It is never actually used (the real collection is `activeCustomers: Map<string, Customer>`), but it is a dead field with a wrong type.\n- `totalRevenue` in `CustomerManager` tracks revenue separately from `EconomySystem.dailyRevenue`. These can diverge since `CustomerManager.recordSale()` takes a `total` parameter but this is only called externally if someone remembers to call it. The register transaction does NOT call `recordSale()` -- it calls `customer.finishCheckout()` directly. So `CustomerManager.totalRevenue` will always be 0.\n- Night customer spawn: `endHour: 2` with `isInTimeRange(23, 2)` works correctly via the midnight-wrap logic. Good.\n\n### 7. CashRegister.ts\n**Purpose:** Checkout state machine (scanning -> payment -> change -> receipt -> anomaly).\n**Key items:** `CashRegister` class, `Transaction` interface, `RegisterState` enum.\n**Issues:**\n- **Customer never finishes checkout on anomaly:** When `triggerAnomaly()` is called (line 173-196), the state changes to ANOMALY and after 2 seconds calls `reset()`. But `reset()` sets `currentCustomer = null` without calling `customer.finishCheckout()`. The customer is now stuck in `CHECKING_OUT` state forever, never completing or leaving. This is a **real bug** -- anomalous transactions leak customers.\n- `findProductShelf()` just picks the first shelf with the product, but if a customer picked from a specific shelf, it may deduct from a different shelf's stock.\n\n### 8. PricingSystem.ts\n**Purpose:** Dynamic pricing with time-of-day demand modifiers and price sensitivity.\n**Key items:** `PricingSystem` class, `updateDemand()`, `willCustomerBuy()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Demand-based product matching uses string includes:** Lines 66-81 check product IDs with `id.includes('water')`, `id.includes('bento')`, etc. This is fragile -- a product named \"water_bottle\" would match the beverage demand modifier incorrectly. Should use category-based matching.\n- `willCustomerBuy()` is never actually called during the customer's shopping flow. Customers pick items regardless of price, making the entire pricing sensitivity system unused.\n\n### 9. EconomySystem.ts\n**Purpose:** Money management, debt tracking, upgrade purchasing, day-end reports.\n**Key items:** `EconomySystem` class, `endDay()`, `isGameOver()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Double-dipping upgrades:** Both `EconomySystem` and `UpgradeManager` track purchased upgrades independently. The `App.ts` applies upgrades from both systems, creating a dual-track that could lead to inconsistencies. If a player buys via `purchaseUpgrade()` (economy) vs `purchaseUpgradeM3()` (upgrade manager), only one system records it.\n- `isGameOver()` checks `dayNumber > DEBT_DAYS` but DEBT_DAYS is 30 and days start at 1, so the check on day 31 is correct. However, the `endDay()` increments dayNumber AFTER the check would normally happen, so the timing is fine.\n\n### 10. ShelfInteraction.ts\n**Purpose:** Player-shelf interaction for stocking/unstocking products.\n**Key items:** `ShelfInteraction` class, `interactWithShelf()`, `getAvailableProductsForShelf()`.\n**Issues:** Minor -- the class is well-structured. No significant bugs.\n\n### 11. DayCycle.ts\n**Purpose:** Day/night cycle with configurable time speeds per phase.\n**Key items:** `DayCycle` class, `DAY_PHASES`, phase change and new day listeners.\n**Issues:**\n- **Time wrap uses hour 30:** `DAY_PHASES` defines danger as 26-29 and dawn as 29-30, then wraps at 30 back to 6. This is internally consistent but means `timeOfDay` ranges from 6 to 30 (not 0-24). The `getPhaseFromTime()` function in GameState.ts handles both conventions but uses `>= 23 || < 2` for NIGHT, which works because the modular time in DayCycle goes 23,24,25,26... then wraps. However, `CustomerManager.isInTimeRange()` uses raw `timeOfDay` values from 0-30 range, which could break spawn schedules if the night phase time values exceed expected ranges.\n- The `getDaylight()` function returns 0 for all hours between 18 and 6, which means there is no gradual dawn/dusk twilight. The GDD specifies gradual transitions.\n\n### 12. DaySummary.ts\n**Purpose:** Daily settlement overlay with income/expense breakdown.\n**Key items:** `DaySummaryManager` class, `render()`, Canvas UI.\n**Issues:** None significant. Clean implementation.\n\n### 13. Lighting.ts\n**Purpose:** 2D lighting system using offscreen canvas with radial gradient light sources.\n**Key items:** `Lighting` class, `getDaylightFactor()`, `getDarknessLevel()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Performance concern:** Every frame, the lighting canvas is resized (`this.lightCanvas.width = width`), which clears the canvas and triggers a re-allocation. This is expensive. The canvas should only be resized when the viewport actually changes.\n- The offscreen canvas is created once but never cached -- gradient objects are created every frame for every light. For 8 lights at 60fps, that is 480 gradient objects per second. Should cache gradients when positions don't change.\n- The `destination-out` composite mode works correctly for cutting light holes in the darkness overlay.\n\n### 14. UpgradeManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Shop upgrade purchase system (lights, storage, register speed, etc.).\n**Key items:** `UpgradeManager` class, upgrade definitions.\n**Issues:**\n- **Duplicate definitions:** Upgrades are defined both here (hardcoded) AND in `upgrades.json` (used by `EconomySystem`). The two lists have different structures and can drift. `UpgradeManager` has 6 items, while `upgrades.json` has 8 (includes radio and flashlight). These are never synchronized.\n\n### 15. SaveManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Auto-save/load to localStorage with system registration.\n**Key items:** `SaveManager` class, `register()`, `save()`, `restore()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Incomplete save:** Only saves M2-M3 systems. M4-M6 systems (HorrorEventManager, JumpScareManager, NPCManager, ClueManager, StoryManager, EndingSystem, RadioManager, NoteManager, NarrativeChoiceManager) are NOT registered with the SaveManager. This means loading a save loses all horror event state, narrative progress, clue discoveries, and story choices.\n- **Two competing save systems:** Both `SaveManager` and `GameState.ts` have `saveGame()`/`loadGame()` functions using different localStorage keys (`midnight_store_save_v3` vs `midnight_store_save`). The App creates both and both run auto-save intervals. This can cause conflicts.\n\n### 16. BehaviorTree.ts\n**Purpose:** Behavior tree system for NPC AI with Selector, Sequence, and action nodes.\n**Key items:** `BTNode`, `Selector`, `Sequence`, `HorrorNPC`, `NPCManager`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Null tree reference:** In `createMysteriousCustomerTree(null!)`, `createLandlordTree(null!)`, and `createHimTree(null!)`, the NPC parameter is `null!` (force-unwrapped null). The tree actions reference `npc.state`, which would throw if `npc` were null. This works only because the closures capture the variable by reference and the NPC is assigned later. However, the `this.npcs` array creates NPCs with `null!` trees but the actual tree is set in the constructor. Wait -- actually, the tree IS passed in the constructor but the `npc` variable used inside closures refers to the outer `null!`. This is a real null pointer issue waiting to happen. The trees close over `npc` which is `null!` at creation time. The closures capture the variable binding, not the value, so when the tree ticks, `npc` is still the parameter value passed at construction... which is `null!`. **This is a bug.** The trees reference `npc.state` but `npc` is the parameter passed as `null!`, not the NPC being constructed.\n- Actually, re-reading: the trees are created as `createMysteriousCustomerTree(null!)` and then passed to the `HorrorNPC` constructor. The tree actions close over the `npc` parameter of the factory function, which is `null!`. These closures will throw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null` when ticked. This is a **confirmed bug**.\n\n### 17. JumpScareManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Jump scare system with daily limits (max 2), preview/scare/fade phases, and consequences.\n**Key items:** `JumpScareManager` class, `JumpScareType` enum, 5 scare types.\n**Issues:**\n- The `updateScare()` method uses `SCARE_DEFS.find(d => d.type === scare.type)!.scareDuration` which could crash if the definition is somehow missing. Minor since the type enum is controlled.\n- Otherwise well-designed. Respects the GDD's 2-scare-per-day limit and cooldown rules.\n\n### 18. HorrorEventManager.ts\n**Purpose:** The core horror system with 20 event types across 4 levels, visual state management, fear regulation.\n**Key items:** `HorrorEventManager` class, `HorrorEventType` enum, `HorrorVisualState`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Fear regulation works but is inverted:** `recentHighFearCount` is incremented when Level 3+ events trigger but is never decremented when time passes without high-fear events. The only reset is `resetDaily()`. Per the GDD, fear should recover gradually during gameplay, not only at day boundaries.\n- **probabilityMod can exceed reasonable bounds:** `dayProgress * 2 * nightDepth * 1.5` on Day 30 in DANGER phase gives `2.0 * 1.5 = 3.0`, tripling all base probabilities. Combined with multiple event types being checked per cycle, this means events fire much more frequently than the GDD's intended rates.\n- Otherwise very comprehensive -- matches the GDD's event list closely, implements buildup/active/resolve phases correctly.\n\n### 19. Renderer.ts\n**Purpose:** Main Canvas 2D renderer handling map, entities, HUD, panels, and horror visual effects.\n**Key items:** `Renderer` class, `render()`, `renderHorrorEffects()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **Screen shake implementation is incorrect (line 683-685):** `c.drawImage(c.canvas, shakeX, shakeY)` draws the canvas onto itself at an offset. This creates feedback artifacts and does not produce proper screen shake. The correct approach is to apply the offset to all world-space rendering, or use `ctx.translate()` before rendering.\n- The renderer is nearly 800 lines and handles too many concerns (HUD, panels, horror effects). This should be split into multiple renderers.\n- The shelf panel does not handle the case where the `ShelfAction` is `STOCK` and the player wants to select which product -- the `stockShelf()` call from the UI is handled externally in `App.ts`, but there is no UI for selecting products within the panel itself.\n\n### 20. NarrativeTypes.ts\n**Purpose:** Shared type definitions for the narrative system.\n**Key items:** `StoryLayer`, `ClueType`, `ClueCategory`, `EndingType`, `NarrativeChoice`, `RadioSegment`, `StoryNote`.\n**Issues:**\n- Typo in `ClueCategory.PREVIOUS_CLERK` -- the value is `'previous_clerck'` (extra 'c'). This mismatch could cause issues if comparing by value rather than enum key.\n\n### 21. StoryManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 6-layer onion narrative progression with day-based layer unlocking.\n**Key items:** `StoryManager` class, layer transition logic.\n**Issues:**\n- **Bug in `acknowledgeEvent()`:** Line 131 filters the event from `pendingEvents`, then line 132 tries to find it in `pendingEvents` again (which was just filtered). The `find()` will always return `undefined`, so `undefined` gets pushed to `storyLog`. Should capture the event before filtering.\n\n### 22. EndingSystem.ts\n**Purpose:** 5 endings (Escape, Takeover, Resist, Merge, Truth) with clue/flag conditions.\n**Key items:** `EndingSystem` class, `evaluate()`, `forceEnding()`.\n**Issues:**\n- The Truth ending requires 65 clues, but `ClueManager` has exactly 75 total clues. Given that many clues have prerequisite chains and are gated behind specific actions (radio, notes), reaching 65 is extremely difficult. The ending conditions may need balancing.\n- Otherwise solid. The reverse-priority evaluation (checking best endings first) is correct.\n\n### 23. NoteManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 22 discoverable story notes across all 6 layers.\n**Key items:** `NoteManager` class, `ALL_NOTES` (22 notes).\n**Issues:** Clean implementation. Notes are well-written and match the GDD's narrative structure.\n\n### 24. NarrativeChoiceManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 10 story choices that set flags and affect endings.\n**Key items:** `NarrativeChoiceManager` class, 10 choices across all layers.\n**Issues:**\n- Choice `choice_final` (Day 30) has 5 options but the game's `forceEnding()` method does not use the final choice's result to determine the ending -- it uses accumulated flags. If the player hasn't set the right flags before Day 30, the final choice is cosmetic only.\n\n### 25. GameState.ts\n**Purpose:** Central game state interface, save/load functions, utility functions.\n**Key items:** `GameState` interface, `createInitialState()`, `getPhaseFromTime()`, `isNightTime()`.\n**Issues:**\n- **getPhaseFromTime() vs DayCycle mismatch:** `getPhaseFromTime()` treats time in 0-24 range, but `DayCycle` uses 6-30 range. For example, `getPhaseFromTime(25)` returns NIGHT (since `25 >= 23`), but in DayCycle, time 25 is in the DANGER phase. Since DayCycle stores its own phase, the `getPhaseFromTime()` in GameState is only used for initialization and should not cause runtime issues, but it is misleading.\n- `cloneState()` uses `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(state))` which will fail if `state` contains `Set`, `Map`, or circular references. The `GameState` interface does not contain these, so it works, but it is fragile.\n\n### 26. ClueManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 75 clues across 6 layers with prerequisite chains and auto-discovery.\n**Key items:** `ClueManager` class, `ALL_CLUES` (75 clues).\n**Issues:**\n- The clue IDs in the data reference IDs like `c_surf_03`, `c_first_01`, etc. These are cross-referenced by `NoteManager`, `RadioManager`, and `NarrativeChoiceManager`. I verified the referenced clue IDs exist in ALL_CLUES. This is correct.\n- `autoDiscoverForDay()` only auto-discovers ENVIRONMENT and CCTV type clues. NOTE, PRODUCT, RADIO, and CUSTOMER clues require explicit discovery, which is correct per the GDD.\n\n### 27. RadioManager.ts\n**Purpose:** 10 late-night radio segments that grant clues.\n**Key items:** `RadioManager` class, `RADIO_SEGMENTS`.\n**Issues:**\n- The `requiredHour` field uses hours in the 0-5 range (for the danger/night phase). But `DayCycle` uses time 23-30, while `RadioManager.update()` uses `timeOfDay % 24`. This means `requiredHour: 3.55` (radio_10) will correctly match `timeOfDay` values around 27.55 when modded to 3.55. This works.\n\n### 28. AudioManager.ts\n**Purpose:** Web Audio API sound system with SFX, ambient loops, BGM, and horror sounds.\n**Key items:** `AudioManager` class, ~20 sound methods, BGM generators.\n**Issues:**\n- **Memory leak:** Ambient and BGM oscillator nodes are pushed to arrays but only cleared on `stopAmbient()`/`stopBgm()`. If `playAmbient()` is called with the same type, it returns early (correct). But if the AudioContext is suspended and resumed, old nodes may not be properly garbage collected.\n- The audio is all synthesized (no sample files), which is fine for MVP but will not match the GDD's audio quality expectations for the final product.\n- BGM is very basic (3 sine waves for daytime, low drones for night). The GDD specifies distinct musical compositions with BPM and instruments. This is placeholder quality.\n\n### 29. main.ts\n**Purpose:** Electron main process with window creation and Steam API stub.\n**Key items:** `createWindow()`, Steam stub, IPC handlers.\n**Issues:**\n- `fullscreen` is imported from electron but never used.\n- Cloud save/load IPC handlers are stubs that do nothing. Fine for MVP.\n- `require('steamworks.js')` is wrapped in try/catch, which is correct for optional dependency.\n\n### 30. preload.ts\n**Purpose:** Electron context bridge exposing limited APIs to renderer.\n**Issues:** None. Minimal and secure.\n\n### 31. App.ts\n**Purpose:** Main game loop wiring all systems together.\n**Key items:** `App` class, `loop()`, system initialization.\n**Issues:**\n- **Two auto-save timers running simultaneously:** `startAutoSave()` (from GameState.ts) and `this.saveManager.startAutoSave()` are both called. They save to different localStorage keys and with potentially different data.\n- **M6 systems not saved:** The SaveManager only registers M2-M3 systems. ClueManager, StoryManager, EndingSystem, RadioManager, NoteManager, NarrativeChoiceManager, HorrorEventManager, JumpScareManager, and NPCManager are NOT registered. Loading a save loses all narrative progress.\n- **Day boundary timing:** `dayCycle.onNewDay()` triggers `this.onNewDay()` which calls `economy.endDay()`. But `economy.endDay()` increments the day counter. Meanwhile `dayCycle` also increments its own day counter. These could diverge since `endDay()` increments `economy.dayNumber` while `dayCycle` increments `this.day`.\n- **Unused purchaseUpgradeM3 method:** Line 693-702 provides an M3 upgrade path but the default is the M2 path. Dual code paths for upgrades is confusing.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Cutting Analysis\n\n### Save/Load Completeness\n**Status: INCOMPLETE.** Only M2-M3 systems are saved. M4-M6 (horror events, NPCs, jump scares, clues, story progress, notes, radio, endings, narrative choices) are all lost on save/load. This is the single biggest gap in the codebase.\n\n### Horror Event Trigger Logic\n**Status: MOSTLY CORRECT** with issues. The event system faithfully implements the GDD's 4-level structure, cooldown system, and sanity-based modifiers. Issues:\n- `recentHighFearCount` never decays during gameplay (only resets at day boundary).\n- Probability multipliers can stack too high (3x on Day 30 danger).\n- Events check all types every 5 seconds, which could cause burst triggers.\n\n### Lighting System Performance\n**Status: ACCEPTABLE for MVP, needs optimization.** The offscreen canvas approach is correct but:\n- Canvas is resized every frame (expensive).\n- Gradient objects are created per-light per-frame.\n- No culling of off-screen lights.\n- The system handles 8 lights at 60fps, which is fine for the current scale but will need optimization if more lights are added.\n\n### Narrative System Completeness\n**Status: COMPREHENSIVE.** The narrative system is the most complete part of the codebase:\n- 75 clues across 6 layers with prerequisite chains\n- 22 discoverable notes\n- 10 narrative choices\n- 10 radio segments\n- 5 endings with flag/clue requirements\n- 6-layer story progression\nAll narrative content is well-written, coherent, and closely matches the GDD's \"onion\" narrative design.\n\n### GDD Compliance\nThe code implements M1-M7 as planned. Notable deviations:\n- GDD specifies Howler.js for audio; the implementation uses raw Web Audio API instead.\n- GDD specifies WebGL for post-processing (glitch shader); the implementation uses Canvas 2D overlays.\n- GDD specifies `steamworks.js`; the implementation has a working stub.\n- GDD specifies `events.json`, `npcs.json`, `clues.json`, `endings.json` data files; only `products.json`, `suppliers.json`, and `upgrades.json` exist. The rest are hardcoded in TypeScript.\n\n### Test Coverage\n**Status: ZERO.** No test files exist. The `tests/` directory is empty. No unit tests, integration tests, or type-check tests were found.\n\n### Architecture Quality\n**Score: 6/10.** The codebase is well-organized into logical modules with clear responsibilities. The main issues are:\n1. Two competing save systems\n2. Dual upgrade tracking (EconomySystem vs UpgradeManager)\n3. NPC behavior tree null reference bug\n4. Customer leak on anomalous transactions\n5. M4-M6 systems not wired into save/load\n6. Renderer is a monolith (800+ lines)\n7. No dependency injection -- systems are tightly coupled via App.ts\n\n### Summary of Bugs Found\n\n| Severity | File | Bug |\n|----------|------|-----|\n| **Critical** | BehaviorTree.ts | NPC trees use `null!` reference -- all NPC behavior will crash on tick |\n| **Critical** | SaveManager.ts | M4-M6 systems not saved; narrative progress lost on load |\n| **High** | CashRegister.ts | Anomalous transactions leave customer stuck in CHECKING_OUT forever |\n| **High** | StoryManager.ts | `acknowledgeEvent()` pushes `undefined` to storyLog |\n| **High** | CustomerManager.ts | `totalRevenue` never updated (register does not call recordSale) |\n| **Medium** | ProductManager.ts | Expiry not updated when mixing old+new stock |\n| **Medium** | Player.ts | `toState()` returns hardcoded zeros for money/debt/health/sanity |\n| **Medium** | GameState.ts + SaveManager.ts | Two competing save systems with different keys |\n| **Medium** | Renderer.ts | Screen shake draws canvas onto itself (feedback artifacts) |\n| **Medium** | NarrativeTypes.ts | Typo `'previous_clerck'` in ClueCategory enum value |\n| **Low** | Customer.ts | Category filtering disabled -- customers browse any shelf |\n| **Low** | PricingSystem.ts | Demand matching uses fragile string includes |\n| **Low** | UpgradeManager.ts | Duplicate definitions with upgrades.json (8 vs 6 items) |\n| **Low** | DayCycle.ts | No gradual dawn/dusk (getDaylight returns 0 for 18-6) |"}],"totalDurationMs":294425,"totalTokens":144719,"totalToolUseCount":41,"usage":{"input_tokens":12045,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":126464,"output_tokens":6210,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":37,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260523181732d4ea6dc134af4329","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both agents have completed their analysis. Now I have comprehensive bug reports for both games. Let me now:\n\n1. Fix the bugs found in both games\n2. Write tests for both games\n3. Verify TypeScript compilation\n4. Generate the test report\n\nLet me start by fixing the critical bugs in both games, then write tests, and finally generate the report.\n\nLet me prioritize the fixes:\n\n**Cultivation Factory - Critical/High bugs:**\n1. Dual Realm Tracking desync (GameState.ts vs Cultivation.ts)\n2. Pill achievement tracking broken (App.ts)\n3. Puppet state machine non-functional (Puppet.ts)\n4. JSON deep clone performance (GameState.ts) - won't fix, architectural\n5. Cross-region transfers duplicate resources (App.ts)\n6. Ascension multiplier not applied (Ascension.ts)\n7. Talisman presets wrong actions (Talisman.ts)\n8. Conveyor glow color parsing bug (ConveyorBelt.ts)\n9. Camera event listener leak (Camera.ts)\n\n**Horror Sim - Critical/High bugs:**\n1. NPC behavior tree null reference (BehaviorTree.ts) - CRITICAL\n2. M4-M6 systems not saved (SaveManager.ts) - CRITICAL\n3. Customer stuck on anomalous transaction (CashRegister.ts) - HIGH\n4. StoryManager acknowledgeEvent pushes undefined (StoryManager.ts) - HIGH\n5. CustomerManager totalRevenue never updated - HIGH\n6. ProductManager expiry not updated - MEDIUM\n7. Player.toState() hardcoded zeros - MEDIUM\n8. Two competing save systems - MEDIUM\n9. Renderer screen shake feedback artifacts - MEDIUM\n10. NarrativeTypes typo - LOW\n\nLet me fix these bugs now. I'll use parallel agents for the two games.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"561a5118-a4d0-464b-8f32-001690e29cb0","uuid":"ea7bd832-a1c1-4c76-8f54-33751bbfda05"}
